    The rules of Levi's Colonization 2026, played by the Classic UI's
    new games: the "classic" rules (the original's) with Roger's
    choices from the rules table (Regeln-Auswahl, "Rogers Wahl" of
    2026-10-06) and his house rules.  A row of the table that is not
    named here plays as "classic".  Rows that play as "freecol":
      1  stockade lock off (the three deletes of the freecol rules)
      5  enhanced missionaries
      8  ships under repair are captured with a colony
     11  colonies may be founded during the war of independence
     12  overflow hammers, crosses and bells are not saved
     13  the player chooses which colonist the school teaches
     17  Pocahontas also lifts the natives' mission bans
    House rules: cancelling a native settlement's question keeps the
    move; no revenge mode (a defeat ends the game); a European is
    defeated when his last colony is lost (from the mandatory colony
    year on, and at once after the declaration), not when his last
    coastal colony is lost; the game goes on open-ended after
    independence (no victory).  The map is the original's New World
    (part P1): its land generator and its world questions LANDMASSE and
    LANDFORM, at the end of this file; its natives (part P2): the
    original's home radius, sizes and no grown land (the native nation
    types below), and its villages (6 to 21 per tribe, as many as the
    land carries, at most 84, the tribes' homes; the options at the end
    of this file).  The original's colony names, leader
    names and Spain's home port come with the names (NameCache), keyed
    by the parent "classic" (mod.xml) and by "levi".

    In case of incompatible changes, please update version number and
    XSD schema for validation.
-->
<freecol-specification id="levi" version="0.120" extends="classic">

  <building-types>
    <!-- Row 1: no stockade lock.  Every colony may be reduced or
         abandoned, whatever its walls, captured ones too.  The fort and
         the fortress copied the stockade's modifier when "classic" was
         read, so all three deletes are needed. -->
    <building-type id="model.building.stockade" preserve="true">
      <modifier id="model.modifier.minimumColonySize" delete="true" />
    </building-type>
    <building-type id="model.building.fort" preserve="true">
      <modifier id="model.modifier.minimumColonySize" delete="true" />
    </building-type>
    <building-type id="model.building.fortress" preserve="true">
      <modifier id="model.modifier.minimumColonySize" delete="true" />
    </building-type>
  </building-types>

  <founding-fathers>
    <!-- Row 17, as the freecol rules. -->
    <founding-father id="model.foundingFather.pocahontas" type="political"
                     weight1="7" weight2="5" weight3="3">
      <modifier id="model.modifier.nativeAlarmModifier"
                type="percentage" value="-50"/>
      <event id="model.event.resetNativeAlarm"/>
      <!-- In Col1 bans on missionaries persist forever.  Allow
           Pocahontas to clear them as a FreeCol extension. -->
      <event id="model.event.resetBannedMissions"/>
    </founding-father>
  </founding-fathers>

  <!-- The original's native land and sizes (part P2).  The original
       (manual, the colony chapter): "Camps and villages have a home
       radius of one square; cities have a home radius of two
       squares"; nothing gives a capital more land.  The populations:
       the original's freshly made games (Roger's «Neue Welt» #13 and
       Inselgruppe #10, 1493; Amerika, 1492) have every settlement, its
       capital too, at the tribe's size: camp 3, village 5, Aztec 7, Inca
       9; its saves from 1505 on have the capitals at 4 / 7 / 10 / 13 and
       the others still at 3 / 5 / 7 / 9 (in Roger's playthrough game 80
       turns on too), so a capital grows once early in the game (when is
       not recorded) and nothing else grows.  Here a capital starts at
       its later size, 4 / 7 / 10 / 13 (FreeCol != original in the first
       years, a question to Roger together with the brave on top; the
       final fixer of part P corrected this comment, which said the
       original starts them so).  FreeCol (classic): capitals one more
       radius (cities 3), 1 to 14 more tiles grown at the start, a brave
       may claim up to 2 tiles beyond the radius, random sizes 4 to 12.
       Here: claimable-radius camp/village 1, Inca/Aztec 2, capitals the
       same; extra-claimable-radius 1, so a brave claims a free tile only
       inside its home radius (ServerUnit: d < radius + 1); minimum =
       maximum size = the original's population; growth 0 (no tiles
       grown).  Everything else is classic's, byte for byte.  The six
       camp and village tribes are copied too: a nation type copies its
       parent's settlement types when it is read, so only a re-read
       picks up the new camp and village.  "default" and "city" stay
       classic's. -->
